#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179360 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Invertible Affine Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "f9d3291b-b95b-536b-93ec-9815f7f554d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12428" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$x = u - v - 2, \\qquad y = v - u + 5$.\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix representing the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $\\det(A) = 0$\n3. The transformation is invertible" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}u - v - 2 \\\\ v - u + 5\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}u - v \\\\ -u + v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)$.\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\det(A) = 0$, the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179361 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "af48b8ba-874a-5753-9874-a2b13fc5a65b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12429" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind $a+b+c$, given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAu + b &= x \\\\\nAu &= x - b \\\\\nu &= A^{-1}(x - b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $\\mathbf T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/((-4) \\cdot 7 - (-9) \\cdot 3)\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A^{-1}) \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(x) - \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = -7 + 3 + 8 = 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179362 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "c5e70edd-5a39-5e85-9461-68df182780dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12430" :question/problem "The affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$x = u - v + 2, \\qquad y = 4u - 5v + 9$.\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ of $\\mathbf T$ is\n$u = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{a}x - y + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{b}, \\qquad v = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{c}x - y + 1$,\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c$?" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAu + b &= x \\\\\nAu &= x - b \\\\\nu &= A^{-1}(x - b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}u - v + 2 \\\\ 4u - 5v + 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}u - v \\\\ 4u - 5v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/(1 \\cdot (-5) - (-1) \\cdot 4)\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A^{-1}) \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(x) - \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x - 2 \\\\ y - 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\cdot (x - 2) + (-1) \\cdot (y - 9) \\\\ 4 \\cdot (x - 2) + (-1) \\cdot (y - 9)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5x - y - 1 \\\\ 4x - y + 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{5}x - y + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{(-1)}, \\qquad v = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{4}x - y + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 5 + (-1) + 4 = 8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179365 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "98ebb2b6-475b-53c5-acae-0b1b0f0778d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-12441" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\nDescribe the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3707/Source/Images/4252f221506b6465a4575a94777c7b53.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation\n$\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b}$,\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0$, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0$, we have two additional subcases:If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(T) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix.\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Invertible Affine Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b312e49-c11e-588b-a6da-f7d730f029b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177545} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ecdb4a-4ac5-5611-bcf3-183d71c424e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177546} {:answer/id #uuid "ca836f28-2336-537f-ba65-f3c8313576b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177547} {:answer/id #uuid "ecec8590-c18f-50b2-a847-a58d55771787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177548} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef5b820-b101-5bad-b752-5e3795e80ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca836f28-2336-537f-ba65-f3c8313576b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177547} :answer-field/id #uuid "597ea9ed-cdf0-5056-886b-c2a14135788d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177544}] :question/id #uuid "267c4ee8-beb7-545b-be5a-57b42fce6778" :question/math-academy-id "q-124111" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$x = u - v, y = u + 2v + 1$.\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $det(A) = 0$\n3. The transformation is invertible"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8378383a-1e39-58e8-942b-1148fba7493f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177617} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7e8148-3e8b-507e-84be-34f0f13a4fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177618} {:answer/id #uuid "a888bd90-ee3d-5094-bda4-c8c9d9ec16a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177619} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a2dd4a-9bc9-558a-b932-9f651af27320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177620} {:answer/id #uuid "29278b62-ecd9-5ef6-9274-3f3d2c544c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29278b62-ecd9-5ef6-9274-3f3d2c544c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177621} :answer-field/id #uuid "575f6f79-762d-5cff-a682-9542260e19eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177616}] :question/id #uuid "b5f8edc9-279a-5eac-9e38-88ca67abf13e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124249" :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind $a + b + c$, given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation $T^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}a & 5 \\\\ b & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d93ef39-d7ad-5fac-bac4-5b86b3ecc463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177623} {:answer/id #uuid "c75631ea-bab4-5ffa-aa69-d37a0aaae467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177624} {:answer/id #uuid "046f5346-80c3-5fb2-8710-4fc3ea285401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177625} {:answer/id #uuid "23607ee1-8add-547c-a0ab-cbb002ff5865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177626} {:answer/id #uuid "2aeaa031-e0be-5321-b218-30939c70fa14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c75631ea-bab4-5ffa-aa69-d37a0aaae467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177624} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9aca0e0-55c1-524b-aaed-ef9b858004f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177622}] :question/id #uuid "8d02964b-863a-50f8-a2b8-3e8d91aa0f8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124317" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$x = 5v - 7u + 3, y = 7v - 10u + 5$.\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation $T^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n$u = ax - 5y + 4, v = bx + cy + 5$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b + c$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0ca5c4c-8587-52d9-ae14-0cbdcbe21018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parallelogram" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178121} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e19566-5405-527d-8374-1d1647216e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parallelepiped" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178122} {:answer/id #uuid "3c951350-a4f2-535f-8dce-20237e848396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178123} {:answer/id #uuid "485f6e67-533a-546a-b57f-9713a9becd12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point on the plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178124} {:answer/id #uuid "7d287e40-1e01-5bc0-a6bd-e3ff70e02485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d287e40-1e01-5bc0-a6bd-e3ff70e02485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178125} :answer-field/id #uuid "a490c4be-601c-5495-90c1-c69a4e9b33a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178120}] :question/id #uuid "79161a5e-44bb-5